<!-- SECTION 8: DOCUMENTS REQUIRED -->
<section class="content-section" id="documents-section">
    <div class="content-container">
        <div class="text-content">
                <h2 class="section-title" id="documents-section">Documents Required for GSTAT Appeal Filing at Ranchi</h2><ul><li>Form APL-05 - GSTAT appeal form (filed electronically on <a href="https://efiling.gstat.gov.in" target="_blank" rel="noopener">efiling.gstat.gov.in</a>)</li><li>Certified copy of impugned order from First Appellate Authority or Revisional Authority</li><li>Pre-deposit payment proof from Electronic Cash Ledger</li><li>GSTIN registration certificate</li><li>Returns (GSTR-1, GSTR-3B, GSTR-9) for disputed period</li><li>Show Cause Notice (SCN) and reply to SCN</li><li>Assessment or adjudication order from original authority</li><li>Vakalatnama in GSTAT FORM-04 stamped per Jharkhand High Court rules</li><li>Affidavit supporting the appeal</li></ul><div class="highlight-box" style="background:var(--orange-light);border-left:4px solid var(--orange);padding:16px 20px;border-radius:var(--radius-md);margin-top:20px;"><p style="font-weight:600;color:var(--blue);margin-bottom:4px;">Jharkhand-specific Requirements</p><p>Mining sector appellants must include mining lease documents, royalty payment records, mineral dispatch registers, and transit pass documentation. Steel businesses should attach HSN classification evidence and input-output reconciliation. Coal sector disputes require coal grade certificates and washery output documentation. See also <a href="/gst-returns">GST return filing</a> for compliance documentation.</p></div>
            </div>
    </div>
</section>

<!-- SECTION 9: CHALLENGES -->
<section class="content-section" id="challenges-section">
    <div class="content-container">
        <div class="text-content">
                <h2 class="section-title" id="challenges-section">Challenges in GSTAT Appeal Filing at Ranchi Bench</h2><h3>Geographic Access</h3><p>With all 24 districts served by a single bench in Ranchi, businesses in the Santhal Parganas region (Dumka, Sahibganj - over 400 km from Ranchi) and Jamshedpur face significant travel. Hybrid mode partially addresses this. See also <a href="/gst-audit">GST audit support</a> for comprehensive compliance review.</p><h3>Dual CGST Commissionerate Structure</h3><p>The Ranchi Commissionerate covers 21 districts while the Jamshedpur Commissionerate covers 3 Singhbhum-region districts. The Dhanbad Sub-Commissionerate (under Ranchi) covers the coal belt. Correctly identifying the respondent is essential.</p><h3>Mining Royalty GST Classification</h3><p>Whether mining royalty constitutes a supply of service subject to GST has been litigated extensively. The Supreme Court's 2024 MADA ruling affects Jharkhand businesses significantly. GSTAT will handle the demand consequences.</p><h3>High-Value Pre-deposit Burden</h3><p>20% under S.112(8) can be disproportionately burdensome for mining and steel companies facing multi-crore demands. Non-payment results in rejection at admission.</p><div class="highlight-box" style="background:var(--blue-lighter);border-left:4px solid var(--blue);padding:16px 20px;border-radius:var(--radius-md);margin-top:20px;"><p style="font-weight:600;color:var(--blue);margin-bottom:4px;">Illustrative Scenario</p><p>A mining company in West Singhbhum receives an Rs 3 crore demand for mining royalty GST under reverse charge from the CGST Jamshedpur Commissionerate. The 20% pre-deposit amounts to Rs 60 lakh. The grounds of appeal must reference the Supreme Court MADA ruling and specific CGST Act provisions. The respondent is the Jamshedpur Commissionerate (not Ranchi). Patron's team handles the precise commissionerate identification and sector-specific drafting.</p></div>
            </div>
    </div>
</section>

