                </aside>
                <div class="faq-expanded__list">
                    <div class="faq-expanded__item" id="faq-1">
                        <h3 class="faq-expanded__q">Which districts fall under the Ranchi GSTAT bench jurisdiction?</h3>
                        <div class="faq-expanded__a"><p>The Ranchi bench covers all 24 districts of Jharkhand. There is no circuit bench. This includes Ranchi, East Singhbhum (Jamshedpur), Dhanbad, Bokaro, Hazaribagh, Giridih, Deoghar, Dumka, Palamu, Garhwa, Godda, Gumla, Chatra, Jamtara, Khunti, Koderma, Latehar, Lohardaga, Pakur, Ramgarh, Sahibganj, Saraikela-Kharsawan, Simdega, and West Singhbhum.</p></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="faq-expanded__item" id="faq-2">
                        <h3 class="faq-expanded__q">Is the Ranchi GSTAT bench operational?</h3>
                        <div class="faq-expanded__a"><p>Yes. Members were allotted via GSTAT Order No. 03/2025 dated 26 December 2025, directed to join 21 January 2026. Technical Member (Centre) Bijoy Bihari Mohapatra posted to Ranchi. GSTAT President Justice Sanjaya Kumar Mishra was formerly Chief Justice of Jharkhand HC. Hearings in hybrid mode.</p></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="faq-expanded__item" id="faq-3">
                        <h3 class="faq-expanded__q">What is the pre-deposit for filing at Ranchi GSTAT bench?</h3>
                        <div class="faq-expanded__a"><p>Pre-deposit under S.112(8) CGST/JGST Act is 20% of disputed tax (cumulative - includes 10% from first appeal). Cap: Rs 20 crore each CGST and SGST. For penalty-only demands, 10%. Non-payment results in rejection at admission. Mining and steel disputes often involve high-value demands requiring careful planning.</p></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="faq-expanded__item" id="faq-4">
                        <h3 class="faq-expanded__q">What is the deadline for filing at Ranchi GSTAT bench?</h3>
                        <div class="faq-expanded__a"><p>3 months from date of order under S.112(1). Condonation under S.112(4) is discretionary. For backlog appeals before 1 April 2026, deadline is 30 June 2026. After 1 April 2026, the standard 3-month limitation applies.</p></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="faq-expanded__item" id="faq-5">
                        <h3 class="faq-expanded__q">What CGST commissionerates operate in Jharkhand?</h3>
                        <div class="faq-expanded__a"><p>Two CGST Commissionerates under Ranchi Zone (HQ at Patna). Ranchi Commissionerate covers 21 districts including Dhanbad Sub-Commissionerate for the coal belt. Jamshedpur Commissionerate covers East Singhbhum, West Singhbhum, and Saraikela-Kharsawan. The correct commissionerate must be named as respondent.</p></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="faq-expanded__item" id="faq-6">
                        <h3 class="faq-expanded__q">Can I appeal a Ranchi GSTAT order to the Jharkhand High Court?</h3>
                        <div class="faq-expanded__a"><p>An appeal under S.113 lies to the Jharkhand High Court at Ranchi on questions of law. The GSTAT is the highest fact-finding authority. Only legal errors can be challenged. The Jharkhand HC is located in Ranchi, making further appeals conveniently accessible.</p></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="faq-expanded__item" id="faq-7">
                        <h3 class="faq-expanded__q">How does the mining royalty GST issue affect GSTAT appeals in Jharkhand?</h3>
                        <div class="faq-expanded__a"><p>The Supreme Court's 2024 MADA ruling confirmed states' power to levy royalty on minerals. The GST classification of mining royalty payments as supply of service under reverse charge has generated significant demand orders in Jharkhand. These disputes will be adjudicated at the Ranchi GSTAT bench, making it a key forum nationally.</p></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="faq-expanded__item" id="faq-8">
                        <h3 class="faq-expanded__q">What is the JGST Act and how does it affect my appeal?</h3>
                        <div class="faq-expanded__a"><p>The Jharkhand GST Act, 2017 is the state legislation mirroring CGST provisions. If your demand was passed by the Commissioner of Commercial Taxes, Jharkhand, the JGST Act applies. The respondent must correctly reflect whether the order was under CGST or JGST. Both acts are substantively identical for appeal purposes.</p></div>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="highlight-box" style="background:var(--gray-50);padding:24px;border-radius:var(--radius-md);margin-top:24px;"><h2 class="section-title" style="font-size:22px;margin-bottom:16px;">Quick Answers - GSTAT Appeal Filing in Ranchi</h2><div class="qa-grid" style="display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(2,1fr);gap:16px;"><div><p style="font-weight:700;color:var(--blue);margin-bottom:4px;">Can I file offline?</p><p>No. Rule 18 mandates online filing via efiling.gstat.gov.in.</p></div><div><p style="font-weight:700;color:var(--blue);margin-bottom:4px;">Is there a circuit bench?</p><p>No. Ranchi is the sole GSTAT bench for all 24 Jharkhand districts.</p></div><div><p style="font-weight:700;color:var(--blue);margin-bottom:4px;">Are Hindi-language orders accepted?</p><p>Yes. GSTAT accepts English and Hindi. Most Jharkhand orders are in English or Hindi - no translation needed.</p></div><div><p style="font-weight:700;color:var(--blue);margin-bottom:4px;">Does Ranchi bench cover Jamshedpur?</p><p>Yes. All Jharkhand districts including East Singhbhum (Jamshedpur) file at the Ranchi bench.</p></div><div><p style="font-weight:700;color:var(--blue);margin-bottom:4px;">Who is the GSTAT President?</p><p>Justice (Retd.) Sanjaya Kumar Mishra, former Chief Justice of the Jharkhand High Court, appointed 6 May 2024.</p></div></div></div>
        <div class="highlight-box" style="background:var(--blue-lighter);border-radius:var(--radius-md);padding:24px;margin-top:32px;">
    <h3 style="color:var(--blue);margin-bottom:16px;">Quick Answers</h3>
    <div class="qa-grid" style="display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(2,1fr);gap:16px;">
        <div><strong>When does NAAR start?</strong><br><span style="color:var(--text-secondary);font-size:14px;">GSTAT Principal Bench acts as NAAR from April 2026 per 56th GST Council recommendation.</span></div>
        <div><strong>What is the NAAR appeal deadline?</strong><br><span style="color:var(--text-secondary);font-size:14px;">30 days from AAAR order under Section 101B. Condonation up to 30 additional days.</span></div>
        <div><strong>Who can file a NAAR appeal?</strong><br><span style="color:var(--text-secondary);font-size:14px;">Any person affected by conflicting AAAR rulings in two or more states.</span></div>
        <div><strong>Is there a pre-deposit?</strong><br><span style="color:var(--text-secondary);font-size:14px;">No  -  only Rs 10,000 filing fee under CGST.</span></div>
        <div><strong>Where is NAAR located?</strong><br><span style="color:var(--text-secondary);font-size:14px;">GSTAT Principal Bench at New Delhi.</span></div>
        <div><strong>Can a single adverse ruling be appealed to NAAR?</strong><br><span style="color:var(--text-secondary);font-size:14px;">No  -  conflicting rulings from 2+ states are required. Single adverse ruling remedy is HC writ.</span></div>
    </div>
